{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1438 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "86072092111c7ef3e85d4b2ce53136795d87f360abb849359b324f491775067e" :edb/db-before-t 1437 :edb/db-after-t 1438 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534750 50 #inst "2026-10-12T10:31:08.771Z" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196458 1078 #edb/ref 17592186196460 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196462 1078 #edb/ref 17592186196464 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196496 1078 #edb/ref 17592186196499 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186196501 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186196502 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186196503 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186196504 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435000 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435001 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435002 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196500 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435003 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186196500 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435000 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198868 1071 "Describe the set $S = {-4,-3,-2,-1, 0, 1, …}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$R$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186198868 1071 "Describe the set $S=\\{-4,-3,-2,-1,0,1,…\\}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x>{{field-2}}\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198868 1074 "The set $S$ contains all integers greater than $-4,$ including $-4.$\n\nIn other words, $S$ contains all integers greater than $-5,$ *excluding* $-5.$ \n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x>-5\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example:\n\n- We can write $x\\ge -4.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x\\ge -4\\}.\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198873 1071 "Describe the set $S = {-5,-10,-20,-40,...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$-\\frac{5}{2} \\cdot 2^{n}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186198873 1071 "Describe the set $S=\\{-5,-10,-20,-40,...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186198873 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n-5,\\,-10,\\,-20,\\,-40,\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-40}{-20} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS=\\{-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287849 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set $\\{-\\frac{1}{2},3\\}$ given below.\n\n$\\{-\\frac{1}{2},3\\}=\\{q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$q\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0\\}$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287849 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${-\\frac{1}{2},3}$ given below.\n\n${-\\frac{1}{2},3}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$q\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186287849 1074 "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${q}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (q-(-\\frac{1}{2}))(q-3) \\\\  & =4(q+\\frac{1}{2})(q-3) \\\\  & =4({q}^{2}-\\frac{5q}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4{q}^{2}-10q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{-\\frac{1}{2},3\\}=\\{q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}-10q-6=0\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287849 1074 "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${q}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (q-(-\\frac{1}{2}))(q-3) \\\\  & =4(q+\\frac{1}{2})(q-3) \\\\  & =4({q}^{2}-\\frac{5q}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4{q}^{2}-10q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{-\\frac{1}{2},3}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}-10q-6=0}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186287853 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set $\\{1\\pm \\text{i}\\}$ given below.\n\n$\\{1\\pm \\text{i}\\}=\\{z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0\\}$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287853 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${1\\pm \\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${1\\pm \\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186287853 1074 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=1-\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=1+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (z-(1-\\text{i}))(z-(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i})z-(1+\\text{i})z+(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i}+1+\\text{i})z+((1)^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(2)z+(1+1)) \\\\  & =2{z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{1\\pm \\text{i}\\}=\\{z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}-4z+4=0\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287853 1074 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=1-\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=1+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (z-(1-\\text{i}))(z-(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i})z-(1+\\text{i})z+(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i}+1+\\text{i})z+((1)^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(2)z+(1+1)) \\\\  & =2{z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{1\\pm \\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}-4z+4=0}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186287864 1071 "Describe the set $S=\\{2,1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287864 1071 "Describe the set $S={2,1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$$\nS={{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186287864 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n2,\\,1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS=\\{4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186287864 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n2,\\,1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N}\n$$" 13194139534750 17592186045417 false] [17592186434999 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435004 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435005 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435006 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435007 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1043 #edb/ref 17592186435008 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1045 "Describing Sets Using Set-Builder Notation" 13194139534750 17592186045417 true] [17592186434999 1046 #edb/ref 17592186435005 13194139534750 1759218604